### SUBTOPIC 1.2: FUNDAMENTAL AND DERIVED QUANTITIES, FUNDAMENTAL AND DERIVED UNITS

---

#### 1. Concept
In physics, we encounter hundreds of measurable physical quantities. However, these quantities are not all independent of one another. For example, speed is calculated by dividing distance by time, and area is obtained by multiplying length by breadth. 

To build a logical, non-redundant system of measurement, physicists define a minimal set of independent physical quantities called **Fundamental (or Base) Quantities** [20–22]. All other physical quantities can then be expressed as mathematical combinations (multiplication and division) of these fundamental quantities; these are known as **Derived Quantities** [20–21].

The corresponding units assigned to fundamental quantities are **Fundamental (Base) Units**, while the units derived from them are **Derived Units**.

---

#### 2. Definition
* **Fundamental (Base) Quantities:** A set of physical quantities that are mutually independent of one another and cannot be defined or expressed in terms of any other physical quantity [20–21].
* **Derived Quantities:** Physical quantities whose definitions depend on one or more fundamental quantities and can be expressed algebraically in terms of base quantities [20–21].
* **Fundamental (Base) Units:** The standard, internationally accepted units defined for measuring fundamental quantities.
* **Derived Units:** Units of measurement for derived quantities obtained by combining fundamental units according to the physical equations defining those quantities.
* **Supplementary Quantities/Units:** Purely geometrical quantities (Plane Angle and Solid Angle) defined in the International System (SI) to complement the base quantities.

---

#### 3. Physical Meaning
The choice of fundamental quantities is a matter of convention, but the minimum number required to cover all of physics is **seven** [21–22]. 

For example, length and time could be chosen as base quantities, making speed a derived quantity (\\(\text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Length}}{\text{Time}}\\)) [20–21]. Alternatively, one could theoretically choose speed and time as fundamental quantities, making length a derived quantity (\\(\text{Length} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time}\\)). The International System of Units (SI) established a standardized framework to ensure global scientific consistency.

---

#### 4. Law / Principle
1. **Principle of Mutual Independence:** No fundamental quantity in a given system of units can be dimensionally represented or derived from the remaining fundamental quantities. The dimension of a base quantity in terms of other base quantities is always zero.
2. **Principle of Dimensional Completeness:** Any derived physical quantity \\(Q\\) can be expressed as a unique product of powers of the base quantities:
\\[Q = M^a L^b T^c I^d K^e (\text{mol})^f (\text{cd})^g\\]

---

#### 5. Equation
For a derived physical quantity \\(Q\\) expressed in terms of SI base units:
\\[u_{\text{derived}} = \text{kg}^a \cdot \text{m}^b \cdot \text{s}^c \cdot \text{A}^d \cdot \text{K}^e \cdot \text{mol}^f \cdot \text{cd}^g\\]

Where \\(a, b, c, d, e, f, g\\) are real numbers (exponents) representing the dimensions of the quantity [28–30].

---

#### 6. Derivation: Step-by-Step Expression of Derived Units from Base Units

##### **A. Force (Newton):**
\\[\text{Force} = \text{Mass} \times \text{Acceleration} = \text{Mass} \times \frac{\text{Velocity}}{\text{Time}} = \text{Mass} \times \frac{\text{Length}}{\text{Time}^2}\\] [28–29]
\\[\text{SI Unit of Force} = \text{kg} \cdot \text{m} \cdot \text{s}^{-2} \equiv \text{Newton (N)}\\] [28–29, 194]

##### **B. Work / Energy (Joule):**
\\[\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Displacement} = (\text{Mass} \times \text{Length} \times \text{Time}^{-2}) \times \text{Length} = \text{Mass} \times \text{Length}^2 \times \text{Time}^{-2}\\]
\\[\text{SI Unit of Work} = \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^2 \cdot \text{s}^{-2} \equiv \text{Joule (J)}\\]

##### **C. Pressure (Pascal):**
\\[\text{Pressure} = \frac{\text{Force}}{\text{Area}} = \frac{\text{Mass} \times \text{Length} \times \text{Time}^{-2}}{\text{Length}^2} = \text{Mass} \times \text{Length}^{-1} \times \text{Time}^{-2}\\]
\\[\text{SI Unit of Pressure} = \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^{-1} \cdot \text{s}^{-2} \equiv \text{Pascal (Pa)}\\] [35–36, 196]

---

#### 7. Meaning of Variables
* \\(M, L, T, I, K, \text{mol}, \text{cd}\\): Representation symbols for Mass, Length, Time, Electric Current, Thermodynamic Temperature, Amount of Substance, and Luminous Intensity respectively.
* \\(a, b, c, d, e, f, g\\): Dimensional exponents (powers).

---

