#### 16. Worked Examples

##### **Worked Example 1.2.1 (Testing Validity of a Base Set)**
**Problem:** Can Force (\\(F\\)), Velocity (\\(V\\)), and Time (\\(T\\)) be chosen as a set of fundamental quantities in mechanics?

* **Solution:**
  * Check if any one of these three quantities can be derived from the remaining two.
  * Acceleration \\(a = \frac{V}{T}\\).
  * Force \\(F = \text{Mass} \times \text{Acceleration} = M \cdot \frac{V}{T} \implies M = \frac{F \cdot T}{V}\\).
  * Mass is required to express Force. None of \\(F, V, T\\) can be expressed solely in terms of the other two without introducing Mass.
  * Therefore, \\((F, V, T)\\) are mutually independent and **CAN** be chosen as a valid set of fundamental quantities [49–50].

##### **Worked Example 1.2.2 (Alternate Base System - H.C. Verma Model)**
**Problem (HCV Ch 1 Ex 5):** If Velocity (\\(V\\)), Time (\\(T\\)), and Force (\\(F\\)) were chosen as basic quantities, find the dimensions of Mass [49–50].

* **Solution:**
  * \\(\text{Force} = \text{Mass} \times \text{Acceleration} = \text{Mass} \times \frac{\text{Velocity}}{\text{Time}}\\) [49–50]
  * \\(F = M \cdot V \cdot T^{-1}\\) [49–50]
  * \\(M = F \cdot T \cdot V^{-1}\\)
  * **Dimensions of Mass:** \\([M] = [F^1 T^1 V^{-1}]\\).

